{
  "dom_id": "step-q308823",
  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "Find where the normal line to the curve $y=\\frac{2}{x}+1$ at the point $(1,3)$ intersects the $x$-axis.\n\nThe normal line intersects the $x$-axis at\n$($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}} $).$",
  "worked_solution": "Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2}{x}+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{-1}+1) \\\\  & =-2{x}^{-2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\nm={f}^{\u2032}(1)=-\\frac{2}{(1)^{2}}=-2,\n$$\n\nand therefore the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{\u2032}=-\\frac{1}{m}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-3 & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-1) \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{1}{2}+3 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we find the point of intersection with the $x$-axis by setting $y=0$ and solving the resulting equation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{5}{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the normal line intersects the  $x$-axis at $(\\,-5,0\\,).$",
